  "notes": "Big-belt pop hardening into pop-rock, straight 2&4 throughout against a half-time parent. The drum grammar is the entry's: \"pop-ballad grammar — kick and reverbed snare on 2&4, half-time in verses opening to full time in the chorus, plus tom fills as section markers; the rock mode uses a driven live kit with crash-on-every-bar.\" The verse/chorus half-time switch is a section device rather than a placement, so it is carried by a wide `kick.densityPerBar` (1—5) and `fourOnFloorInChorus`, while the tom cascades are `tom`, `tomHigh`, `tomLow` and `crash` in the perc lanes with the snare-roll ladder off — the entry says fills are toms, not rolls. The snare carries a large reverb tail. No `bass808` block: \"no 808. Sustained synth or electric bass on roots, doubling the kick pattern\", so the bassline is a kick mirror at 0.94. Harmony is the entry's stated pair, \"i—VI—III—VII and i—VII—VI are the workhorses\", carried at the top two weights. Melody encodes \"wide-interval and ascending, built to a held note, with gospel-derived runs at cadences\" as a leap-weighted interval spread, an arch contour, `long_arc` and `endVariation`; \"gang-vocal or choir stacks for the last chorus\" is a `sustain_pad` counter. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says the parameters are \"lane-conventional\": every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 82 (the midpoint of the first-named 70—95 power-ballad core) are INFERRED. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the belt-register claim is repeated here as the reason the topline ceiling is a reading rather than a measurement.",
